<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-arithmetic">
  <title>Operations</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-number-operations"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Use the four arithmetic operations to solve problems.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine whether each operation is commutative and associative.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain when multiplication or division distributes over a grouped sum or difference.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Addition, subtraction, multiplication and division each have their own properties. Brackets and other grouping symbols reveal the structure of an expression.</p></introduction>

  <section xml:id="arithmetic-intro">
    <title>Operations and Their Properties</title>
    <p>The four arithmetic operations are <term>addition</term>, <term>subtraction</term>, <term>multiplication</term> and <term>division</term>. They model combining, comparing, grouping, scaling and sharing. A group is a single mathematical object for the purpose of an operation; brackets, a fraction bar and a square-root bar can all indicate grouping.</p>
    <p>An operation is <term>commutative</term> when swapping the operands does not change the result. It is <term>associative</term> when regrouping repeated uses of that operation does not change the result. These are separate properties.</p>
    <table xml:id="operation-properties">
      <title>Properties of the four operations</title>
      <tabular>
        <col width="23%"/><col width="23%"/><col width="23%"/><col width="31%"/>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Operation</cell><cell>Commutative?</cell><cell>Associative?</cell><cell>Distributive relationship</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Addition</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Not the standard distribution law</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Subtraction</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>Not in general</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Multiplication</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Yes</cell><cell>Over addition and subtraction</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Division</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>Across a sum or difference in the numerator only</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>For example, <m>3+5=5+3</m> and <m>(2+3)+4=2+(3+4)</m>. By contrast, <m>8-3\ne3-8</m> and <m>(12-6)-2\ne12-(6-2)</m>. Division also fails both commutativity and associativity in general.</p>
    <p><term>Distribution</term> relates two operations. Multiplication distributes over a grouped sum: <m>a(b+c)=ab+ac</m>. It also distributes over a grouped difference: <m>a(b-c)=ab-ac</m>. For example, <m>3(4+5)=3\cdot4+3\cdot5</m>.</p>
    <p>Division distributes over a grouped numerator when the same non-zero divisor is applied to every term: <m>\frac{a+b}{c}=\frac{a}{c}+\frac{b}{c}</m>, for <m>c\ne0</m>. This does <em>not</em> mean that division distributes over a denominator: <m>\frac{12}{2+4}=2</m>, whereas <m>\frac{12}{2}+\frac{12}{4}=9</m>.</p>
    <p>Adding a number to a group, such as <m>3+(4+5)</m>, does not multiply it into every term. Here the brackets may be removed because addition is associative. It is therefore essential to name the principle used, not just the visible method.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="arithmetic-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-arithmetic">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Evaluate 7+3×4.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Multiplication is performed first: 7+12=19.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-arithmetic">
      <statement><p>Evaluate (7+3)×4 and explain why it differs.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The brackets group the addition first, giving 10×4=40.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
